﻿@* LoginLayout.razor — Phase 0.11
   Per roadmap Concern A: real branded full-screen layout (NOT a pass-through).
   - Full-screen dark gradient background (overrides the main Radzen theme)
   - Centered card container for the Login.razor child
   - TakOne brand mark + tagline at the top
   - Footer with copyright + language switcher link

   Login.razor itself becomes a child of this layout (it sets
   @layout LoginLayout) and just renders the form fields inside the card. *@

@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

<div class="login-shell">
    <div class="login-bg"></div>

    <main class="login-card">
        <header class="login-brand">
            <h1>TakOne</h1>
            <p>@Localizer["Tagline"]</p>
        </header>

        @Body

        <footer class="login-footer">
            <span>&copy; @(CultureFormat.FormatDigits(DateTime.UtcNow.Year)) TakOne</span>
            @* Language switcher — writes the `takone_culture` cookie in the
               format CookieRequestCultureProvider expects (`c=X|uic=X`), then
               force-reloads so the localization middleware re-evaluates.
               MUST match MainLayout.razor's OnCultureChanged exactly so the
               two switchers stay in sync.

               IMPORTANT — WHY PLAIN HTML onclick + JS (not Blazor @onclick):
               Login.razor and ChangePassword.razor are STATIC SSR pages (no
               Blazor circuit). Blazor's @onclick handler requires a live
               circuit to round-trip the click event to the server — with no
               circuit, @onclick silently never fires. This is the same
               reason the eye-toggle button on those pages uses plain
               onclick="takTogglePw(this)" instead of @onclick.

               The inline takSwitchCulture() function at the bottom of this
               file sets the cookie via document.cookie and force-reloads
               via window.location.reload() — pure browser, no server
               round-trip needed. Works identically on /Account/Login and
               /Account/ChangePassword (both static SSR) AND on any future
               page that adopts this layout.

               Previous version used `@onclick="SwitchCultureAsync"` — that
               was the bug: clicks were silently ignored on /Account/Login
               and /Account/ChangePassword because no circuit existed. The
               `@inject IJSRuntime JS` + `Navigation.NavigateTo(Uri,
               forceLoad: true)` pattern only works when a circuit is
               established (the JSRuntime needs a circuit to dispatch
               through). With no circuit, the @onclick never fires AND the
               JSRuntime call would also fail — but we never got that far
               because the click itself was the broken link in the chain.

               The href="javascript:void(0)" keeps the <a> semantically a
               link (cursor: pointer, focusable, keyboard-accessible) while
               the onclick handler does the actual work. *@
            <a href="javascript:void(0)"
               onclick="takSwitchCulture('en-US')">@Localizer["SwitchToEnglish"]</a>
            <a href="javascript:void(0)"
               onclick="takSwitchCulture('fa-IR')">@Localizer["SwitchToPersian"]</a>
        </footer>
    </main>
</div>

@* ── Inline culture-switch script ──
   Sets the `takone_culture` cookie in the format ASP.NET Core's
   CookieRequestCultureProvider expects (`c=XX|uic=XX`), then force-reloads
   so the localization middleware re-evaluates on the next request.

   The cookie name MUST match `CookieRequestCultureProvider.DefaultCookieName`
   ("takone_culture" is configured in Program.cs via
   options.CookieName = "takone_culture"). The value format
   `c={culture}|uic={culture}` is the standard CookieRequestCultureProvider
   format — `c` is the culture (used for formatting dates/numbers), `uic`
   is the UI culture (used for resource lookups). We set both to the same
   value because we don't differentiate them in this app.

   The max-age=31536000 (1 year) + path=/ + samesite=strict attributes
   match what MainLayout.razor's SwitchCultureAsync sets via JSRuntime.
   samesite=strict is the security default — the cookie is only sent on
   same-site requests, so a malicious site can't trick the user's browser
   into switching their TakOne language by linking to a TakOne URL.

   WHY INLINE (not a separate .js file):
     - The function is 3 lines of trivial cookie + reload — too small to
       justify a separate file, an extra HTTP request, a registration in
       App.razor, and a cache-bust version parameter.
     - It's layout-local: only the LoginLayout uses it (the main app's
       MainLayout has its own SwitchCultureAsync via JSRuntime because
       the main app DOES have a Blazor circuit).
     - It MUST run without a Blazor circuit (see the comment above), so
       it can't use JSRuntime — inline browser JS is the only option.

   Identical in spirit to MainLayout.SwitchCultureAsync — kept duplicated
   because the two layouts live in different worlds (MainLayout always
   has a circuit; LoginLayout never does). Trying to share a single
   implementation would require either (a) JSRuntime (broken without a
   circuit) or (b) a shared <script src> in App.razor (loads the function
   on every page even when LoginLayout isn't used). The 3-line
   duplication is the lesser evil. *@
<script>
    function takSwitchCulture(culture) {
        var cookieValue = 'c=' + culture + '|uic=' + culture;
        document.cookie = 'takone_culture=' + cookieValue + '; path=/; max-age=31536000; samesite=strict';
        window.location.reload();
    }
</script>

@code {
    [Inject] private IStringLocalizer<LoginLayout> Localizer { get; set; } = default!;
}